GNU bash 5.3.20 for DOS (DJGPP, 32-bit, needs DPMI: vmdos's own), on C: only
(the boot floppy has no room) as C:\VMDOS\BASH.EXE.

  C:\>bash
  bash-5.3$ for f in /vmdos/vm*.com; do echo "${f##*/}"; done
  bash-5.3$ mem | find "Free" | while read a b; do echo "[$a]"; done
  bash-5.3$ v=$(command.com /c ver); echo "${v^^}"

The current release, built from GNU's git (savannah, master at "Bash-5.3
patch 20"), not the old DJGPP ports (2.05, 4.4): readline 8.3 line editing
with history, tab completion, Ctrl-R, arrays and associative arrays, [[ =~ ]],
$(...), functions, traps, here-documents, brace expansion, printf -v, ...

  bash-5.3-djgpp.patch    the changes to bash's own sources (about 100 lines)
  dos/dosproc.c           fork, pipes, exec, Ctrl-C and the keyboard for DOS
  dos/dosfork.S           the register half of fork
  dos/dosbash.h           included first in every file: SIGCHLD and such
  COPYING                 bash's license, GPL version 3

Nothing prebuilt is kept: make builds deps/bash/BASH.EXE with tools/build-bash.sh
(DJGPP gcc 12, -O2 -march=i486, UPX --best) from bash's git at a pinned commit
(GNU's savannah, or a mirror), fetching the DJGPP cross compiler into deps/djgpp
(tools/get-djgpp.sh) unless one is on PATH. CI keeps deps/ in its cache.

How it runs on DOS
------------------
DOS runs one program at a time and has no fork(), and bash forks for every
subshell, pipeline, $(...) and external command. dos/dosproc.c gives it one:

  fork()   saves the shell's memory (data, heap and the live part of the
           stack: about 300 KB) to a DPMI block and carries on as the child.
           When the child ends, the memory is copied back, the parent's file
           handles and current directory are put back, and fork() returns
           again, in the parent. The child has always finished by then, so
           wait just collects its status.
  pipes    a temporary file in %TMPDIR%, %TMP% or %TEMP% (else X:\TMP on the
           current drive). The writer runs to the end, then the reader reads.
  execve   spawns the program and ends the child with its exit status.
           #!/bin/sh and #!/bin/bash scripts are run by the shell itself;
           other interpreters (#!/usr/bin/perl, #!/usr/bin/env python) are
           looked for in PATH.

What that means in use:

  - Pipelines run one stage after another, not side by side. Output goes
    through a file, so a command that never ends (yes | head) never ends.
  - No job control: & runs the command to the end before the prompt comes
    back; there's no fg/bg/Ctrl-Z. No coprocesses, no <(...) or >(...), no
    /dev/tcp.
  - DOS programs are found with their extension: `mem' runs MEM.EXE, `x'
    runs X.COM, X.EXE or X.BAT (in that order), and .BAT files run through
    COMMAND.COM. DOS programs get the DOS command line limit (126 chars);
    DJGPP programs don't.
  - Ctrl-C works as on Unix: at the prompt it clears the line, in a loop it
    stops the loop, and in a program it ends the program (and the loop it is
    in).

Paths and names
---------------
  PATH is kept the Unix way inside the shell (C:/BIN:C:/:C:/VMDOS; a
  drive letter's colon isn't a separator) and handed to programs the DOS way
  (C:\;C:\VMDOS;C:\VMDOS\NET).
  C:/DOS and C:\DOS (quoted) are absolute paths; cd C:/VMDOS changes drive.
  Globs and completion ignore case, as DOS does (shopt nocaseglob is on).
  SHELL is this bash if not set (exported), so DJGPP programs run their
  commands (awk's system() and pipes) in bash rather than COMMAND.COM.
  HOME is the current drive's root if not set; ~/.bashrc there needs long
  file names (DOSLFN, loaded by AUTOEXEC.BAT); _bashrc is not looked for.

COMMAND.COM's own commands (DIR, COPY, DEL, TYPE, VER, ...) aren't programs:
run them as `command.com /c dir' (plain `command' is bash's builtin). For
example, in ~/.bashrc:  dir () { command.com /c dir "$@"; }

The Unix tools (ls, cp, rm, mkdir, cat, grep, sed, find, xargs, sort, ...)
are in C:\BIN: third_party/sbase/README.vmdos; and awk (GNU awk 5.4):
third_party/gawk/README.vmdos. bash puts C:/BIN first on
its PATH (C being the drive bash is on), ahead of DOS's FIND and SORT.
